var tafsirData_58 = {
  "1": "\"Indeed, Allah has heard the argument of the woman who pleaded with you O Prophet concerning her husband, and appealed to Allah. Allah has heard your exchange. Surely Allah is All-Hearing, All-Seeing.\" opens a new chapter with intimate divine attention to a single voice. After Surah al-Hadid's cosmic themes of iron, balance, and infinite bounty, the Quran now shows that the All-Wise who knows what is hidden in hearts is also the One who hears a worried woman's plea about her family.\n\nThe woman is identified in authentic tradition as Khawlah bint Tha'labah, who came to the Prophet distressed because her husband Aws ibn al-Samit had pronounced \"you are to me like the back of my mother,\" a form of divorce called \"zihar,\" which in pre-Islamic custom was treated as final separation yet left the woman suspended without rights. She argued, pleaded, and appealed to Allah because she feared for her children and household, saying her husband had consumed her youth and wealth and now left her in need. She raised her voice in the Prophet's home while 'Aisha, the Prophet's wife, could barely hear from a nearby room, yet Allah heard from above the heavens.\n\n\"Indeed, Allah has heard\" is repeated for emphasis, first regarding the woman's argument and then regarding the exchange between her and the Prophet. Repetition affirms that no word was missed and that divine justice is attentive even when human systems are slow. \"Argument of the woman who pleaded\" translates \"mujadala,\" the same root as the chapter's title, indicating earnest discussion, not quarrel.\n\n\"Surely Allah is All-Hearing, All-Seeing\" provides the theological anchor. All-Hearing encompasses loud debates and whispered appeals; All-Seeing encompasses private hardship and the Prophet's thoughtful listening. That a chapter of law begins with hearing a woman's grievance teaches that revelation cares for vulnerable individuals, not only for empires balancing Scripture and iron.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to reduce this to a story about divorce alone. Its wider lesson is that Allah hears appeals, that the Messenger is a safe place for grievances, and that law should protect those whose voices are easily overlooked. For practice, the verse encourages listening as the Prophet listened, advocating for those suspended between status categories, and turning directly to Allah with pleas, confident that the Near, All-Hearing responds even when the room is crowded.",
  "2": "\"Those of you who sinfully divorce their wives by comparing them to their mothers should know that their wives are in no way their mothers. None can be their mothers except those who gave birth to them. What they say is certainly detestable and false. Yet Allah is truly Ever-Pardoning, All-Forgiving.\" corrects the harmful practice of \"zihar\" immediately after affirming that Allah heard the woman's plea. The verse addresses husbands who pronounce this formula as a form of divorce or estrangement.\n\nThe phrase \"by comparing them to their mothers\" captures the literal wording \"you are to me like the back of my mother,\" equating a lawful spouse with a permanently forbidden relative. The Quran declares the reality plainly. \"Their wives are in no way their mothers. None can be their mothers except those who gave birth to them.\" Saying it does not make it so. Biology, kinship, and sacred law are not changed by angry utterance. The statement strips away the illusion that words alone can transform sacred bonds.\n\n\"What they say is certainly detestable and false\" names the moral quality of the utterance. \"Detestable\" translates \"munkar,\" an act rejected by sound norms and revelation, and \"false\" translates \"zur,\" a lie or deviation used to justify injustice. Together they show that zihar is not merely poor manners, but a wrongful, dishonest manipulation of family law that suspends a woman unjustly and harms children.\n\nYet the verse does not close with despair. \"Yet Allah is truly Ever-Pardoning, All-Forgiving\" opens a way to repair. Despite the detestable falsehood, sincere repentance, expiation, and proper rectification can restore the marriage and erase sin. This balance mirrors the Quran's pattern of naming wrong clearly while inviting return, as seen earlier where those who took revelation lightly were invited to glorify and to lend the good loan.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to apply this only to ancient phrasing. The principle extends to using sacred relationships as threats or jokes to control a spouse. Marital speech should be truthful and kind, not coercive.\n\nPractically, the verse teaches careful speech in family life, protection of women's dignity, and trust that law guided by the All-Hearing and All-Seeing aims to heal homes, not merely to punish. The prescribed expiations that follow show that words have weight, but forgiveness remains available to those who correct course.",
  "3": "\"Those who divorce their wives in this manner, then wish to retract what they said, must free a slave before they touch each other. This penalty is meant to deter you. And Allah is All-Aware of what you do.\" lays down the first level of expiation for \"zihar\" after declaring it detestable and false yet forgivable. The verse recognizes human regret and provides a path to repair that honors the seriousness of what was said.\n\n\"Those who divorce their wives in this manner, then wish to retract what they said\" identifies husbands who pronounced the hurtful comparison and later desire to resume normal marital relations. The law does not dismiss their wish to reconcile, but it requires that reconciliation pass through a meaningful act of repair before intimacy resumes. The phrase \"before they touch each other\" sets a clear boundary, showing that words about the body have consequences for the body and that haste is not permitted where dignity was harmed.\n\n\"Must free a slave\" was, in its historical context, a profound act of liberation, returning a person to freedom and family. It also addressed a social structure where slavery existed, directing believers toward emancipating rather than exploiting. Classical jurists note that if a slave is not available to free, as in later times when slavery is abolished and outlawed, the alternatives specified in the next verse, fasting or feeding the poor, apply. The wisdom remains: liberation or, where not applicable, equivalently weighty discipline and generosity.\n\n\"This penalty is meant to deter you\" clarifies purpose. The expiation is not humiliation, but deterrence and education. By requiring a costly, beneficial act, the law teaches couples to avoid using sacred kinship as a weapon and to weigh words before wounding.\n\n\"And Allah is All-Aware of what you do\" closes with awareness that matches the opening \"All-Hearing, All-Seeing.\" Allah sees whether the required act is performed sincerely or evaded, whether reconciliation is sought with good intent or manipulation, and whether the wife is treated with restored honor.\n\nA modern reader may see freeing a slave as distant, yet its ethic translates into freeing people from other bonds through charity, education, debt relief, or anti-exploitation efforts where appropriate and lawful. The central lesson endures. Speech in marriage matters enough that its harm requires tangible repair, and the One who heard the woman's plea ensures that repair benefits the vulnerable rather than merely excusing the offender.",
  "4": "\"But if the husband cannot afford this, let him then fast two consecutive months before the couple touch each other. But if he is unable to fast, then let him feed sixty poor people. This is to re-affirm your faith in Allah and His Messenger. These are the limits set by Allah. And the disbelievers will suffer a painful punishment.\" details the graduated alternatives when freeing a slave is not possible, completing the law of \"zihar\" reconciliation.\n\nThe first alternative, \"fast two consecutive months before the couple touch each other,\" requires sixty days of uninterrupted fasting, a demanding discipline that tests sincerity and self-control. Classical jurists explain that continuity matters, so breaking the fast without valid excuse restarts the count, emphasizing that words about family cannot be treated lightly. The requirement of consecutiveness teaches that hasty appetites should not override reflective repair. Fasting also revives the hardened heart, as earlier verses noted revival of dead earth and hearts, turning indulgence that spoiled some into restraint that refines.\n\n\"But if he is unable to fast, then let him feed sixty poor people\" offers a third option for those genuinely unable to fast due to age, illness, or other compelling cause. Feeding sixty poor people turns a private marital wrong into public benefit, feeding many vulnerable families. Early reports indicate the Prophet allowed this with dates or similar staple, stressing that feeding should be sufficient and dignified, not symbolic scraps.\n\n\"This is to re-affirm your faith in Allah and His Messenger\" states the spiritual purpose. Expiation is not merely a fine, but a re-affirmation. By accepting the limit, the husband demonstrates that he honors revelation even when it costs, aligning provision with thanks rather than with denial.\n\n\"These are the limits set by Allah\" translates \"hudud Allah,\" boundaries that protect families and society. Just as earlier chapters described balance and iron for justice, these limits provide balance for homes, protecting wives from suspended status and husbands from careless speech while still allowing sincere reconciliation.\n\nThe warning \"And the disbelievers will suffer a painful punishment\" contrasts those who respect these limits with those who deny them. It reminds that denying divine law after having heard it carries consequence beyond domestic repair. For present life, the law's spirit remains. Guard marital speech, take prompt, sincere steps to repair harm, and remember that the All-Aware who heard the woman's plea continues to hear and see how families honor or neglect His limits set for their protection.",
  "5": "\"Indeed, those who defy Allah and His Messenger will be debased, just like those before them. We have certainly sent down clear revelations. And the disbelievers will suffer a humiliating punishment.\" widen the lesson from family law to communal defiance. After detailing the limits for \"zihar\" and urging re-affirmation of faith, the Quran warns that opposing Allah and His Messenger leads to debasement, following the pattern of earlier deniers.\n\nThe word \"defy\" translates \"yuhaddun,\" meaning to set oneself on the opposing border or side against Allah and His Messenger, as if drawing a line and standing deliberately against the order that Scripture and balance were sent to establish. Debasement, or \"kubitu,\" suggests being struck down, disgraced, and suppressed after arrogance, like a plant crushed after swelling. The comparison \"just like those before them\" shows continuity. Pharaoh, 'Ad, Thamud, and those who mocked earlier messengers were debased despite wealth and strongholds, and the present defiers are not exempt because they live later or speak more politely.\n\n\"We have certainly sent down clear revelations\" affirms that defiance is not excused by obscurity. The same noble Quran described as being in a well-preserved Record, sent from the Lord of all worlds to bring people from darkness to light, has been made clear. The revelation is not hidden; the choice to oppose it is conscious.\n\n\"And the disbelievers will suffer a humiliating punishment\" adds that debasement in this world may precede, and certainly leads to, a humiliating punishment in the next. The adjective humiliating matches the crime. Those who sought honor through defiance receive humiliation, just as earlier the luxury-spoiled were debased while the foremost were elevated.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read this as a call to humiliate others personally. The Quran assigns debasement and punishment to Allah's judgment, often executed through just means and ultimately through the Hereafter, not through vigilante shaming. For believers, the practical restraint is to avoid the path of defiance itself, which includes promoting stinginess, secret conspiracy in sin, or using oaths as shields. Instead, stand with the party of Allah through faith, justice, and mercy, remembering that humiliation is the fitting end of defiance, while honor lies in humble submission to the clear revelations already sent down.",
  "6": "\"On the Day Allah resurrects them all together, He will then inform them of what they have done. Allah has kept account of it all, while they have forgotten it. For Allah is a Witness over all things.\" describes the comprehensive accounting that follows debasement for defiance. After stating that clear revelations have been sent, the verse moves to the gathering where revelation's response will be reviewed.\n\n\"On the Day Allah resurrects them all together\" echoes the earlier gathering of earlier and later generations for the appointed Day. \"All together\" stresses that no defier is missed and no private conspirator hides. Those who met in threes and fives thinking they were unseen, those who greeted the Prophet with twisted words, and those who made oaths as shields will be present alongside the believers who gave room in gatherings and were elevated.\n\n\"He will then inform them of what they have done\" shows justice as clarification, not mere sentencing. People will be shown their deeds with context, intention, and consequence, so that even the denier recognizes truth. This informing counters the self-deception that \"we have something to stand on\" when swearing falsely on that Day.\n\nThe crucial phrase \"Allah has kept account of it all, while they have forgotten it\" contrasts perfect preservation with human forgetting. While they forgot secret talks, broken trusts, and false greetings, Allah kept precise account, as with the well-preserved Record that guards the noble Quran. Forgetting does not erase liability, just as a borrower forgetting a loan does not cancel repayment. The record preserves what memory dropped.\n\n\"For Allah is a Witness over all things\" closes with scope. A witness sees and can testify. Human witnesses are limited, but divine witness encompasses speech, silence, and hidden intent, as earlier verses said He knows whatever is in heavens and earth and knows what is hidden in the heart.\n\nSome may read this as frightening surveillance. The Quran frames it as fairness before that Day arrives. Because accounting is perfect, punishment will not be arbitrary and reward will not be withheld. For daily life, remembering this accounting motivates careful speech in gatherings, honesty in oaths, and prompt repentance for what has been forgotten, trusting the All-Aware Witness to remember for the sake of perfect justice and to forgive what is sincerely corrected.",
  "7": "\"Do you not see that Allah knows whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth? If three converse privately, He is their fourth. If five, He is their sixth. Whether fewer or more, He is with them wherever they may be. Then, on the Day of Judgment, He will inform them of what they have done. Surely Allah has perfect knowledge of all things.\" expands the witness of the previous verse into a vivid teaching about private conversation.\n\nThe opening question, \"Do you not see,\" invites reflection rather than demanding blind acceptance. It points to observation of the heavens and earth that already glorify Allah as Almighty, All-Wise. If the kingdom of heavens and earth belongs to Him, and He is with you wherever you are, then intimate knowledge follows naturally.\n\nThe core image is striking. \"If three converse privately, He is their fourth. If five, He is their sixth. Whether fewer or more, He is with them wherever they may be.\" The counting suggests divine withness in knowledge and witnessing, not that Allah is physically counted among them. Even the smallest secret gathering, two people whispering where they assume no one hears, includes divine hearing and seeing. Whether three, five, fewer, or more, location does not limit this. The private room is not private from the All-Hearing, All-Seeing, who is with you wherever you are, as stated in Surah al-Hadid.\n\n\"Then, on the Day of Judgment, He will inform them of what they have done\" connects present privacy to future publicity. Secret talks about sin, aggression, or disobedience to the Messenger and also secret talks for goodness and righteousness will be brought forward with clarity. The same Lord who kept account while they forgot will now display it.\n\n\"Surely Allah has perfect knowledge of all things\" seals the teaching. \"Perfect knowledge\" means knowledge that is encompassing and detailed, not approximate.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to imagine divine withness as spying that makes trust impossible, or to misuse the verse to police others' privacy. The verse teaches self-regulation, not surveillance of fellow believers. It encourages choosing private speech carefully, as the next verse will command, so that when the Day's informing comes, one's private record matches one's public claim, and the soul is greeted with peace rather than exposed with regret.",
  "8": "\"Have you not seen those who were forbidden from secret talks, yet they always return to what they were forbidden from, conspiring in sin, aggression, and disobedience to the Messenger? And when they come to you O Prophet, they greet you not as Allah greets you, and say to one another, “Why does Allah not punish us for what we say?” Hell is enough for them—they will burn in it. And what an evil destination!\" exposes conspiratorial behavior among hypocrites and their allies in Medina. After establishing that Allah hears private conversations, the verse shows a group that persisted despite being clearly forbidden.\n\n\"Those who were forbidden from secret talks, yet they always return\" describes obstinate disobedience. They were instructed not to use private gatherings for harm, yet they habitually circled back, forming small huddles that whispered about sinning against others, acting aggressively beyond limits, and disobeying the Messenger who had been sent to bring people from darkness to light. Secret talk itself is not sinful; the content makes it so, as the next verse will clarify about talks for goodness and righteousness.\n\nTheir greeting, \"they greet you not as Allah greets you,\" deepens the picture. Instead of the dignified \"salam\" that conveys peace, they used distorted greetings, twisting \"as-salamu 'alayka\" into \"as-samu 'alayka,\" meaning death be upon you, a subtle insult delivered with a smile. When confronted in heart, they did not repent. They said among themselves, \"Why does Allah not punish us for what we say?\" taking delay in punishment as proof that their insult was trivial or that the Prophet was not truly guided, rather than as mercy giving time to repent.\n\n\"Hell is enough for them—they will burn in it. And what an evil destination!\" answers their challenge. Delay is not absence. The punishment they mocked as absent is prepared and sufficient, not excessive or deficient, precisely fitting persistent mockery.\n\nA contemporary parallel is the small, encrypted chat or hallway whisper that plans harm, spreads rumors, or undermines trustworthy leadership while maintaining polite public appearance. The Quran does not forbid privacy, but warns against using it to grieve believers.\n\nThe lesson is to use private communication transparently, to greet with genuine peace, and to treat delay in consequences as opportunity to correct rather than as permission to continue, seeking refuge from a destination described as truly evil.",
  "9": "\"O believers! When you converse privately, let it not be for sin, aggression, or disobedience to the Messenger, but let it be for goodness and righteousness. And fear Allah, to Whom you will all be gathered.\" gives the positive standard for private speech after exposing conspiratorial misuse. The direct address \"O believers\" distinguishes those who wish to be among the party of Allah from those who chose defiance and shielding oaths.\n\nThree forbidden aims are listed. \"Sin\" includes acts wrong in themselves, such as lying, backbiting, and conspiring to harm. \"Aggression\" includes transgressing against others' rights, plotting to seize, intimidate, or exclude. \"Disobedience to the Messenger\" includes undermining the clear proofs, Scripture, and balance sent for justice and the counsel brought to bring people from darkness to light. Private talk for any of these, even if cleverly phrased or technically deniable, is prohibited because the withness of Allah in private remains complete.\n\nThe alternative, \"but let it be for goodness and righteousness,\" offers the permitted path. \"Goodness\" translates \"birr,\" broad, generous excellence toward people, including supporting the needy, reconciling families, and encouraging honest work. \"Righteousness\" translates \"taqwa,\" reverent mindfulness that keeps commitments to Allah, keeping limits set for marriage and gatherings. Private conversation should therefore plan charity, reconcile disputes, and strengthen obedience to the Messenger, not weaken it.\n\n\"And fear Allah, to Whom you will all be gathered\" adds accountability as motivation. Fear here is reverent caution, remembering the gathering of earlier and later generations on the appointed Day where secret talks will be disclosed. The One who merges night and day and knows what is hidden in the heart will gather both the whisper and the whisperer.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this forbids any private discussion. The verse does not. It forbids private discussion aimed at harm and encourages private coordination for good, like planning a good loan, arranging help for a vulnerable woman like the pleader at the chapter's opening, or advising a leader sincerely.\n\nPractically, before starting a private chat, one can ask whether its purpose is sin, aggression, disobedience, or goodness and righteousness. Choosing the latter turns privacy from a shield for wrong into a shelter for mercy, and embodies the trust called for earlier, to put trust in Allah who cannot be deceived by closed doors.",
  "10": "\"Secret talks are only inspired by Satan to grieve the believers. Yet he cannot harm them whatsoever except by Allah’s Will. So in Allah let the believers put their trust.\" diagnoses the source and limit of conspiratorial whispering. After commanding private talk for goodness, the verse explains why harmful secrecy works on the heart and why it ultimately fails.\n\n\"Secret talks are only inspired by Satan to grieve the believers\" identifies the whisperer behind the whisper. \"Najwa,\" private talk, when turned toward sin, aggression, and disobedience, reflects satanic suggestion that capitalizes on anxiety. In Medina, hypocrites and their allies would fall silent when believers approached, exchange glances, and whisper, causing companions to fear attack or betrayal. The same pattern repeats today when small groups huddle conspicuously or when messages are deliberately withheld to make others feel excluded and fearful.\n\nThe verb \"to grieve\" indicates the aim is sadness, worry, and suspicion rather than immediate physical harm. Satan cannot force a hand, but he can paint thoughts that make believers imagine the worst, suspect one another, and hesitate to give room in gatherings or to lend the good loan.\n\nThe remedy follows. \"Yet he cannot harm them whatsoever except by Allah's Will\" removes illusion of satanic omnipotence. No whisper can override divine decree. If protection is permitted, it reaches; if harm is permitted, it carries wisdom and trial, never arbitrary cruelty, as the Record principle teaches that every calamity or blessing is written before creation.\n\nTherefore, \"So in Allah let the believers put their trust.\" Trust, or \"tawakkul,\" is reliance that combines using lawful means with entrusting outcome to Allah. Rather than responding to whispers with more whispers, surveillance, or paranoia, believers answer with balanced reliance, communicating clearly for goodness, making room generously, and continuing to establish prayer and give charity.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to dismiss all anxiety as satanic while ignoring practical steps. Trust includes seeking clarification when worried, verifying news, and protecting gatherings with dignified order, not naivete.\n\nRemembering this verse helps a community refuse to be grieved. Choose private talk for goodness, assume good of fellow believers where appropriate, verify before suspecting, and place trust where the kingdom truly belongs, with Allah, the All-Powerful, Almighty.",
  "11": "\"O believers! When you are told to make room in gatherings, then do so. Allah will make room for you in His grace. And if you are told to rise, then do so. Allah will elevate those of you who are faithful, and raise those gifted with knowledge in rank. And Allah is All-Aware of what you do.\" teaches etiquette that turns gatherings into extensions of justice and mercy. After addressing private talks, the Quran now addresses public space.\n\n\"When you are told to make room in gatherings, then do so\" recalls a Medina situation where early companions crowded near the Prophet to hear clear proofs and where latecomers, often people of insight and need, found no place. Being asked to shift, tighten, or move to make room was not an insult but an act of fairness allowing all to share in guidance and balance. The command sounds small, yet it trains humility. A puffed self clings to a seat; a believing self slides over.\n\nThe promise \"Allah will make room for you in His grace\" links a small physical adjustment to wide divine relief. \"Make room\" translates \"yafsah,\" to expand, to give ease and spaciousness. The recompense includes relief from hardship, forgiveness, and spacious provision in the Gardens where thrones and elevated furnishings await, as described for the people of the right.\n\n\"And if you are told to rise, then do so\" addresses a second instruction, whether to stand for prayer, to go forth in a campaign that protects justice, to give space for those with greater right, or to leave a gathering when appropriate. Rising can mean moving from comfort to duty, and obedience there also brings elevation.\n\n\"Allah will elevate those of you who are faithful, and raise those gifted with knowledge in rank\" states the principle behind the promises. Elevation is not random or based on ancestry, as earlier verses about Noah and Abraham clarified. Faith elevates, and knowledge joined to faith elevates further. The believer who makes room demonstrates faith; the knowledgeable believer demonstrates understanding that harming a brother's access for a seat is not worth a cramped heart.\n\n\"And Allah is All-Aware of what you do\" closes with assurance that even this small shift of the body is seen and measured. No courteous move is forgotten by the Witness who keeps account while people forget, and who will inform them on the Day when all are gathered with light or without it.",
  "12": "\"O believers! When you consult the Messenger privately, give something in charity before your consultation. That is better and purer for you. But if you lack the means, then Allah is truly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\" introduces a disciplined etiquette for private access to the Prophet. After teaching public manners about making room, the verse now refines private manners.\n\nIn Medina, the Prophet was accessible and compassionate, so many sought private counsel for personal, family, or community matters. Frequent, casual consultations could consume time needed for revelation, teaching, and leading, while also crowding out those with urgent needs. Requiring charity before consultation sifted motives, ensuring that private time was sought for genuine goodness and righteousness rather than for sin or display. It also transformed private speech into an act that immediately benefited the poor, linking consultation to the good loan principle.\n\n\"Give something in charity before your consultation\" made charity a gateway to conversation. The amount was not fixed as burdensome; early reports indicate it could be as little as a coin or handful of dates, but its presence proved sincerity and provided for those who had settled in the city and loved the emigrants yet could be in need.\n\n\"That is better and purer for you\" explains benefit. Better, because it elevates the consultation from mere talk to worship, and purer, because it cleanses the heart from using access for vanity or harm. The requirement purifies intention and wealth alike.\n\n\"But if you lack the means, then Allah is truly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\" ensures no one sincere is blocked. The door remains open for those unable to give, reflecting the broader Quranic balance where limits are firm yet mercy accompanies genuine inability, as seen with alternatives for \"zihar\" expiation from freeing a slave to fasting to feeding sixty poor.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think charity buys access. The verse teaches the opposite. Charity refines access and soon, as the next verse shows, this specific requirement was lifted when the lesson was learned, leaving prayer, alms, and obedience as ongoing practices.\n\nFor today, while private consultation with the Messenger is no longer directly possible, the ethic remains. When seeking time with teachers or leaders, come with clear purpose, bring benefit to others where possible, and be mindful of shared resources, seeking the better and purer way that Allah loves to see.",
  "13": "\"Are you afraid of spending in charity before your private consultations with him? Since you are unable to do so, and Allah has turned to you in mercy, then continue to establish prayer, pay alms-tax, and obey Allah and His Messenger. And Allah is All-Aware of what you do.\" acknowledges the believers' hesitation after the previous directive and then lifts the specific requirement while confirming enduring duties.\n\nThe question \"Are you afraid of spending\" uncovers the heart's worry. Some companions, including Ali, gave charity before consulting, yet others feared that frequent need for advice would mean repeated charity they could not sustain, or that they would appear lacking compared to wealthier companions. Fear here is not cowardice, but anxiety about adequacy and about being prevented from seeking guidance. The Quran names the feeling gently to normalize it.\n\n\"Since you are unable to do so, and Allah has turned to you in mercy\" announces relief. The specific obligation to give before each private consultation was lightened. Tradition notes that only one companion acted on the original command before it was eased, showing how quickly the community internalized the lesson about valuing the Prophet's time and purifying intention. Mercy does not cancel learning; it reduces burden once learning is achieved, similar to how expiation scales from freeing a slave to fasting to feeding.\n\n\"Then continue to establish prayer, pay alms-tax, and obey Allah and His Messenger\" redirects to permanent pillars. Establishing prayer guards the humility that private success can undo, paying obligatory charity sustains the poor beyond occasional gifts before talks, and obeying Allah and His Messenger encompasses the broader call to bring people from darkness to light and to administer balance and iron justly. What was temporary for consultation becomes continuous for character.\n\n\"And Allah is All-Aware of what you do\" closes again with encompassing knowledge. He is aware who consulted for genuine goodness versus display, who gave despite fear, who felt unable, and who now prays and pays alms consistently.\n\nA modern application is to balance access to guidance with generosity and discipline. Seek counsel purposefully, support the community's needs through regular alms, and remember that mercy eases what would become hardship while still honoring the original lesson that private access should serve goodness and righteousness, not sin or self-promotion, under the awareness of the All-Aware.",
  "14": "\"Have you not seen those hypocrites who ally themselves with a people with whom Allah is displeased? They are neither with you nor with them. And they swear to lies knowingly.\" exposes a political alliance rooted in hypocrisy. After teaching believers to fear Allah, give charity, and purify private talks, the verse turns to those who did the opposite by aligning with the displeased while pretending to be with believers.\n\n\"Those hypocrites who ally themselves with a people with whom Allah is displeased\" refers in the Medina context to hypocrites who secretly allied with Jewish tribes who had rejected the Messenger despite recognizing clear proofs, and more broadly to any group that knowingly defies Allah and His Messenger after guidance came. Alliance here means taking them as confidants, sharing believers' secrets, and hoping for strength from their side while maintaining public claim to faith.\n\n\"They are neither with you nor with them\" captures their in-between status, belonging fully to neither camp because loyalty is instrumental, not principled. This mirrors the wall scene where the tormented are separated from the graced. In life their ambiguity seemed clever; on the Day they will swear, thinking they have something to stand on, and be exposed as total liars.\n\n\"And they swear to lies knowingly\" identifies method. Oaths are used as shields to hinder from Allah's way while concealing true allegiance. They swear they are with believers, swear they did not conspire, swear after being seen at forbidden secret talks. The knowledge qualifier, \"knowingly,\" stresses that this is not mistaken recollection but deliberate falsehood, a trait the next verses will say Satan decorates and then abandons.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to use this verse to declare every political contact with a displeasing group as hypocrisy. The Quran later distinguishes kindly, fair dealing with those who did not fight or expel believers from alliances that betray trust and religion. Evaluation looks at loyalty, betrayal, and lying, not merely at contact.\n\nFor today, the verse warns against double allegiance that uses faith for social benefit while privately working against its interests, and cautions against protecting any alliance with knowing lies. Integrity requires choosing a side honestly, treating all people justly, and refusing to offer false oaths that corrode both personal record and communal trust under the witness of the All-Aware.",
  "15": "\"Allah has prepared for them a severe punishment. Evil indeed is what they do.\" follows the exposure of hypocrites who allied with a displeased people and swore to lies knowingly. The verse moves from description to recompense, showing that private alliances and false oaths do not vanish when forgotten by people.\n\n\"Allah has prepared for them a severe punishment\" uses the verb \"prepared,\" or \"a'adda,\" meaning readied, made ready in advance and kept in place. The preparation is certain and is attributed directly to Allah as Owner of the kingdom and the appointed Day. Severity indicates intensity and proportion to wrong. It is more than discomfort, because using oaths as shields, hindering others from Allah's way, and allying with defiance harms community trust more than an isolated lie.\n\n\"Evil indeed is what they do\" then evaluates the present moral quality of their actions, not only the future punishment. The Quran does not say only that punishment is severe, but that what they do is evil, indicating that the deeds themselves are corrupting. Hindering from Allah's way, misusing the name of Allah in false swearing, and cultivating double loyalty each spreads the stain beyond the actor.\n\nThis pairing of severe punishment and evil deed mirrors earlier pairings where debasement followed defiance and humiliating punishment followed disbelief. Evil deeds are not described neutrally as strategies; they are named as evil to invite immediate turning, just as harsh outcomes are named to convey that turning is urgent.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to assume severe punishment contradicts the earlier emphasis on Allah being All-Forgiving, Most Merciful, or Ever-Pardoning, All-Forgiving. The Quran holds both truths together. Forgiveness remains possible before the soul reaches the throat for those who turn sincerely, while severity remains the just outcome for those who persist knowingly after clear proofs, balance, and repeated invitations to humble hearts at remembrance.\n\nFor practical reflection, remembering preparation should motivate gentle courage. Rather than joining an alliance that requires lying, a believer can stand with truth plainly, make room for the faithful, give the good loan openly, and refuse to offer or accept false oaths, trusting that the Severe in preparation is also the Merciful who prepared forgiveness for the returning heart and light for the sincere.",
  "16": "\"They have made their false oaths as a shield, hindering others from the cause of Allah. So they will suffer a humiliating punishment.\" details how alliance with falsehood operates in practice. After noting that allies of displeased people swear to lies knowingly and that severe punishment is prepared for them, the verse now explains the instrument and effect of that lying.\n\n\"They have made their false oaths as a shield\" describes using sworn statements not to affirm truth but to protect scheming. In Medina, hypocrites would swear they were sincere believers, swear they had not attended forbidden secret talks, and swear they had not mocked, all to obtain trust, access, and immunity from censure. The oath, which in Islam is meant to solemnly invoke Allah as witness to truth, is inverted into a cover that deflects scrutiny while work against believers continues. The shield image conveys defensiveness and manipulation. It looks like piety, but functions as armor for plotting.\n\n\"Hindering others from the cause of Allah\" reveals the outward harm. By presenting themselves as insiders, these hypocrites could discourage donation, cast doubt on the Messenger's invitation, whisper that provision will be lost if spent, and suggest that spending before victory is wasteful. Fellow believers hearing a confident oath might hesitate on the path that brings from darkness to light, exactly the path the noble Quran was sent to illuminate.\n\n\"So they will suffer a humiliating punishment\" matches the humiliation inherent in the crime. They sought to humiliate sincere believers through grieving secret talks and to elevate themselves through shields of falseness, therefore humiliation becomes the fitting recompense. The earlier verse about severe punishment now specifies its character as humiliating, countering the illusion that shields provide dignity.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think any oath is suspect. The Quran distinguishes solemn, truthful oaths kept with integrity from false oaths used as shields. Truthful oaths upheld build trust and justice; false shields destroy it.\n\nFor today, the verse teaches careful speech. Do not offer oaths lightly, do not use religious language to deflect legitimate accountability, and be wary when someone repeatedly shields behavior with emphatic swearing while discouraging good. Choose instead private talk for goodness and righteousness, make room in gatherings sincerely, and let yes be yes under the awareness of the All-Aware who will inform all of what they did on the Day when shields no longer hold.",
  "17": "\"Neither their wealth nor children will be of any help to them against Allah whatsoever. It is they who will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever.\" strips away the two supports hypocrites relied upon to sustain double alliances and false shields. After describing severe and humiliating punishment for using oaths to hinder, the verse declares the uselessness of worldly means on the Day.\n\n\"Neither their wealth nor children\" names the most cherished forms of protection. In Arabian and many modern contexts, wealth buys influence, lawyers, and delay, while children and kin provide networks, defenders, and reputation. Both can fund secret talks, finance iron, or create the appearance of strength behind strongholds and walls. The Quran states they will be of no help \"against Allah whatsoever,\" meaning they cannot avert judgment, purchase ransom, or alter the informing that Allah will give of what was done. The earlier verse \"So Today no ransom will be accepted\" and the later warning about the sieve of debt in this life converge here.\n\n\"It is they who will be the residents of the Fire\" identifies the outcome. \"Residents\" indicates lingering, not passing through. The earlier description of severe and humiliating punishment now receives its locale. The Fire is the only fitting home for those who made falsehood a residence in life.\n\n\"They will be there forever\" indicates permanence for those who persist in hypocrisy and disbelief until death without return. The Quran's pattern holds that forgiveness remains open before the throat moment and through sincere repentance, but persistence unto death fixes residence.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think the verse devalues wealth or family in this life. The Quran elsewhere praises wealth lent as a good loan and children raised with faith as delight and responsibility. What is negated is their ability to shield against Allah's reckoning, not their value as trusts to be used well.\n\nPractically, this verse reorders reliance. Build provision, support family, and seek beneficial iron and knowledge, but do not imagine they substitute for truthful oaths, private talk for goodness, and humility at remembrance. Invest wealth and nurture children in ways that will be recognized positively when only faith, truthful witness, and divine mercy provide actual help on the Day.",
  "18": "\"On the Day Allah resurrects them all, they will falsely swear to Him as they swear to you, thinking they have something to stand on. Indeed, it is they who are the total liars.\" portrays the unchanging habit of false swearing carried even into the resurrection. After stating that wealth, children, and shielding oaths cannot help, the verse shows that old tactics are attempted before Allah Himself.\n\n\"On the Day Allah resurrects them all\" returns to the gathering of earlier and later generations for the appointed Day. \"Them all\" includes hypocrites who allied with displeased people and thought their calculated ambiguity would protect them. \"They will falsely swear to Him as they swear to you\" shows that the pattern is not dropped. In life they swore to believers to deflect blame; on that Day they swear to Allah, saying they were believers, that they did not hinder, that their secret talks were innocent. They imagine that confident speech can still shape reality, as it sometimes does among people who cannot see hearts.\n\n\"Thinking they have something to stand on\" translates \"yahsabuna annahum 'ala shay',\" imagining they possess valid ground, an argument, a shelter. The verb reveals self-deception. After years of using oaths as shields, they have come to half-believe their own shields are solid. Yet the One they now address is He who kept account while they forgot and who knows what is hidden in the heart, so the shield is paper before witness.\n\n\"Indeed, it is they who are the total liars\" gives the divine verdict. \"Total liars\" intensifies the charge, indicating completeness and centrality of lying to their character, not merely occasional slips. This echoes the earlier exposure \"they swear to lies knowingly,\" now confirmed at the highest court.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read this as suggestion that lies may sometimes succeed before Allah. The verse demonstrates the opposite. Success before people through lying does not transfer to success before the All-Witness, whose record is the well-preserved one no false hand can touch.\n\nFor daily conduct, the verse warns against making lying habitual. A liar begins by deceiving others and ends by deceiving himself, thinking his standing is secure. Truthfulness, especially in private talks and oaths, builds genuine standing that does not need to swear falsely to anyone, let alone to the One who is with you wherever you are and will inform you of what you did.",
  "19": "\"Satan has taken hold of them, causing them to forget the remembrance of Allah. They are the party of Satan. Surely Satan’s party is bound to lose.\" explains how persistent falsehood and hindered paths reach their spiritual root. After showing that swearing falsely continues even at resurrection and is exposed as total lies, the verse discloses the captor behind the habit.\n\n\"Satan has taken hold of them\" translates \"istahwadha,\" meaning to dominate, encompass, and take mastery, like an owner rounding up livestock. It is stronger than a passing whisper. Through repeated choices to use oaths as shields, to prefer secret talks of sin, and to ally with displeasure, they opened the way for Satan to gain dominance, not by compulsion that removes responsibility, but by cultivated habit that invites takeover.\n\n\"Causing them to forget the remembrance of Allah\" identifies the first damage of that dominance. Forgetting remembrance includes neglecting Quranic recitation, prayer, humble heart at remembrance, and the balance that revelation brought. Earlier the Quran asked, \"Has the time not yet come for hearts to be humbled at remembrance,\" and warned that hearts become hardened when time stretches. Forgetting remembrance is the mechanism of that hardening. When remembrance is forgotten, wealth, children, oaths, and strongholds take its place as imagined protectors.\n\n\"They are the party of Satan\" places them within an affiliation. Parties in the Quran include the party of Allah, who are bound to succeed, and the party of Satan, who are bound to lose. Party membership is shown by loyalty, not by name. Those who love to grieve believers with secret talks, promote stinginess, and hinder from Allah's way have enlisted, whether they admit it or not.\n\n\"Surely Satan's party is bound to lose\" is both prediction and reassurance. Loss includes failure in this world where their conspiracies from fortified strongholds and behind walls ultimately fracture, as later verses show, and loss in the next where the Fire is the only fitting home.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to view possession as excuse. The Quran keeps responsibility intact. Satan decorates and deceives, but humans choose to follow.\n\nPractically, breaking hold begins with renewed remembrance, establishing prayer, reading clear revelations, making room in gatherings sincerely, and lending the good loan, thereby rejoining the party that is bound to succeed rather than persisting with the party bound to lose.",
  "20": "\"As for those who defy Allah and His Messenger, they will definitely be among the most debased.\" restates the outcome for defiers after the diagnosis that Satan has taken hold and made them forget remembrance. The verse mirrors the earlier statement that those who defy Allah and His Messenger will be debased like those before them, now intensified with \"definitely\" and \"most debased.\"\n\n\"Defy\" again translates standing on the opposing side, drawing a boundary against the Scripture and balance sent so that people may administer justice and against the iron given to support just order. Defiance includes persisting in using oaths as shields, hindering from the path, conspiring in aggression, and mocking the Messenger's invitation to believe.\n\n\"Will definitely be among the most debased\" adds certainty and degree. \"Definitely\" conveys emphatic fulfillment, removing any thought that alliances with fortified peoples or wealth and children might lift them. \"Most debased\" translates \"adhalin,\" the lowest, most abased ranking, far below the elevation promised to those who are faithful and those gifted with knowledge. While believers are elevated in rank and given light ahead and to the right, defiers sink to the lowest standing, the opposite of the foremost's nearness.\n\nThis debasement occurs in both worlds. In this world, conspiracies fracture, hearts divide, and strongholds do not protect, as verses about cowardice and disunity will detail. In the next, severe, humiliating punishment and a Fire without ransom await, while no ransom will be accepted. The pattern shows that the party that chose Satan's mastery becomes the most debased, while the party of Allah becomes the successful.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to apply debasement as license for believers to humiliate individuals personally. The Quran assigns rank with Allah, who is All-Powerful, Almighty, and whose decree ensures that He and His messengers will prevail. Believers are not tasked with public shaming but with upholding balance, spreading clear proofs, and continuing to make room and give charity.\n\nFor personal transformation, the verse invites leaving the opposing side. No lineage, partnership, or wealth makes defiance safe. Turning to the side of the Messenger through humble remembrance, truthful oaths, private talk for goodness, and standing for justice moves one, by divine favor, from the predicted debasement to the promised elevation that Allah grants to those He wills.",
  "21": "\"Allah has decreed, “I and My messengers will certainly prevail.” Surely Allah is All-Powerful, Almighty.\" announces the unalterable decree that underlies every previous warning and promise. After stating that defiers will be most debased and that Satan's party is bound to lose, the verse gives the positive counterpart, the guaranteed success of Allah and His messengers.\n\n\"Allah has decreed\" translates \"kataba,\" meaning He has written, inscribed, and firmly ordained. Writing here recalls the well-preserved Record and the writing of every calamity and blessing before it occurs. This particular writing is a decree of outcome. It is not a wish, but a fixed inscription that no conspiracy, secret talk, or alliance behind walls can erase.\n\nThe content of the decree, \"I and My messengers will certainly prevail,\" uses first person that conveys direct divine voice and personal commitment. Prevailing includes the evident triumph of the message itself, which brings people from darkness to light, and the historical and ultimate vindication of those who carry it, whether through open victory, through preservation of truth against distortion, or through the decisive prevailing on the appointed Day when all matters are returned. The earlier note that those who donated and fought before the victory have greater rank presumes that a victory does come, by this decree.\n\n\"Surely Allah is All-Powerful, Almighty\" gives the attribute that secures the decree. \"All-Powerful\" is \"Qawiyy,\" possessing complete strength that does not tire, while \"Mighty\" is \"'Aziz,\" whose might cannot be repelled. Together they assure that no iron, stronghold, wealth, or number can prevent the decree's fulfillment, just as no one can prevent death or bring back a soul that has reached the throat.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read prevailing as guarantee that every personal desire of believers will be granted quickly. The Quran clarifies that prevailing may involve hardship, patience, and the test of standing up for Allah without seeing Him, but the final outcome favors the party of Allah, who are bound to succeed.\n\nFor daily life, this decree stabilizes reliance. Rather than relying on shifting alliances and false shields, believers can stand for balance, give the good loan, and speak truthfully in private and public, trusting that the Decreeing, All-Powerful, Almighty will bring His and His messengers' cause to prevail in the Most Wise way and timing.",
  "22": "\"You will never find a people who truly believe in Allah and the Last Day loyal to those who defy Allah and His Messenger, even if they were their parents, children, siblings, or extended family. For those believers, Allah has instilled faith in their hearts and strengthened them with a spirit from Him. He will admit them into Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there forever. Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Him. They are the party of Allah. Indeed, Allah’s party is bound to succeed.\" defines the loyalty of true faith and its reward. After decreeing that He and His messengers will prevail as the All-Powerful, Almighty, the verse shows what prevailing looks like in personal allegiance.\n\n\"Loyal to those who defy Allah and His Messenger\" translates \"muwadda,\" deep affection and alliance that takes the opposing side as intimate protectors. True believers do not combine trust in the Last Day with affectionate alliance to defiance, even when blood ties pull strongest. Parents, children, siblings, and extended family are named precisely because those bonds most naturally incline a heart to compromise. The verse does not forbid kindness, fairness, or family care, as elsewhere the Quran commands kind dealings even with those who do not fight or expel believers. It forbids taking defiers as the chosen intimate party while claiming faith, the same double allegiance condemned in hypocrites who swore to lies.\n\n\"For those believers, Allah has instilled faith in their hearts and strengthened them with a spirit from Him\" describes divine support that makes such loyalty possible. Faith is not merely asserted; it is engraved by Allah, steadying the heart that earlier needed revival. The spirit, or \"ruh,\" is reassurance, light, and strength that enables standing without seeing Him.\n\nThe reward restates Gardens under which rivers flow forever, but adds mutual pleasure: \"Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Him,\" the highest relational achievement. Finally, they are named \"the party of Allah\" who are bound to succeed, contrasting with the party of Satan who are bound to lose. Their success is therefore not measured by immediate worldly dominance, but by enduring divine pleasure and the Gardens that follow.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to use this verse to justify harshness toward non-believing relatives. The Quran elsewhere requires maintaining family ties with kindness while maintaining firm loyalty to truth, a balance that shows love without compromising core allegiance, trusting that the All-Aware judges intentions perfectly."
};